1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,360
Kiran Maya Sheikh graduated with a
computer science degree in 2025.

2
00:00:04,880 --> 00:00:06,600
She wanted stability, financial

3
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:10,280
independence, and a first full-time job as
a software engineer.

4
00:00:10,920 --> 00:00:14,280
Finding it, she later explained, was
harder than she expected.

5
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:18,640
Kiran described a frustrating paradox:
some internships

6
00:00:18,640 --> 00:00:22,840
and entry-level jobs asked for experience
before offering a chance to gain it.

7
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:25,560
She kept building her skills, volunteered

8
00:00:25,560 --> 00:00:28,360
on a project, and looked for a
professional community.

9
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:30,520
She believed AI was one

10
00:00:30,560 --> 00:00:33,960
factor making an already difficult market
harder for beginners.

11
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:36,720
Her experience alone cannot tell us how

12
00:00:36,720 --> 00:00:41,160
much AI changed her chances, or whether it
caused any particular rejection.

13
00:00:41,680 --> 00:00:45,560
But AI can now help with some of the tasks
beginners once learned on.

14
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:47,080
Not every task.

15
00:00:47,280 --> 00:00:48,360
Not every job.

16
00:00:48,880 --> 00:00:51,160
Still, it raises a frightening question:

17
00:00:51,760 --> 00:00:55,400
what happens if the first rung of the
career ladder starts to disappear?

18
00:00:56,080 --> 00:00:57,600
A warning sign appeared in an

19
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:01,720
unexpected place: a company building the
technology itself.

20
00:01:02,240 --> 00:01:07,040
In October 2026, Anthropic reported that
the share of junior roles among its

21
00:01:07,040 --> 00:01:12,760
technical hires had fallen from 27 percent
to 9 percent since early 2025.

22
00:01:13,240 --> 00:01:16,120
That doesn't mean nine out of ten junior
jobs vanished.

23
00:01:16,480 --> 00:01:18,600
It doesn't tell us how many people were
fired.

24
00:01:19,160 --> 00:01:23,467
Anthropic was growing, and one company is
not the whole economy.

25
00:01:23,467 --> 00:01:27,400
But the change raises a question bigger
than one office: if fewer

26
00:01:27,440 --> 00:01:31,400
beginners get the chance to practice,
where will tomorrow's experts come from?

27
00:01:32,080 --> 00:01:35,400
For generations, a career often worked
like an apprenticeship.

28
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:38,053
First you did the smaller tasks.

29
00:01:38,053 --> 00:01:40,480
You made mistakes someone else could
catch.

30
00:01:40,480 --> 00:01:44,640
Over time, those tasks taught you how the
whole job worked.

31
00:01:44,640 --> 00:01:48,587
Now imagine an experienced worker with an
AI assistant.

32
00:01:48,587 --> 00:01:50,440
The assistant drafts a document,

33
00:01:50,480 --> 00:01:54,400
searches for information, or produces a
first attempt at code.

34
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:57,467
The human checks it and makes the final
decisions.

35
00:01:57,467 --> 00:02:02,160
If that arrangement lets a team take on
more work, it could create opportunities.

36
00:02:02,160 --> 00:02:03,920
But if a company needs fewer people

37
00:02:03,920 --> 00:02:07,960
for those early tasks, it may also hire
fewer beginners.

38
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:10,160
And that creates a paradox.

39
00:02:10,160 --> 00:02:13,013
Businesses still want experienced people.

40
00:02:13,013 --> 00:02:15,733
But experience takes somewhere to begin.

41
00:02:15,733 --> 00:02:17,360
If enough companies stop providing

42
00:02:17,360 --> 00:02:21,440
those first opportunities, the shortage
may not appear immediately.

43
00:02:21,440 --> 00:02:23,280
It could appear years later,

44
00:02:23,280 --> 00:02:27,360
when the people who should have learned
those skills were never given the chance.

45
00:02:27,360 --> 00:02:28,920
Researchers have also started

46
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:32,360
testing what AI assistance might mean for
expertise itself.

47
00:02:32,480 --> 00:02:36,160
In a three-month experiment involving 133
patent

48
00:02:36,160 --> 00:02:40,600
lawyers, those given an AI assistant
produced better drafts on average.

49
00:02:41,320 --> 00:02:43,000
Later, on a separate task they

50
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:46,640
were instructed to complete without AI,
experienced lawyers who

51
00:02:46,640 --> 00:02:50,680
had previously had access scored higher
than their peers in the control group.

52
00:02:51,280 --> 00:02:55,160
Among junior lawyers, researchers found no
clear average difference.

53
00:02:55,600 --> 00:03:00,040
This was a working paper, not a verdict on
every profession: the study was

54
00:03:00,120 --> 00:03:05,640
small, focused on one occupation, and only
91 participants completed the final task.

55
00:03:06,200 --> 00:03:09,080
Some findings became less certain in a
sensitivity check.

56
00:03:09,520 --> 00:03:14,120
It does not prove that AI stops beginners
from learning or makes them lose skills.

57
00:03:14,560 --> 00:03:19,240
The career-ladder scenario is a possible
chain of events, not a proven forecast.

58
00:03:19,640 --> 00:03:21,240
And the evidence so far is

59
00:03:21,240 --> 00:03:23,880
more complicated than the loudest
headlines suggest.

60
00:03:24,320 --> 00:03:28,920
In a study published in March 2026,
Anthropic researchers found no

61
00:03:28,960 --> 00:03:34,040
systematic increase in unemployment among
workers in occupations more exposed to AI.

62
00:03:34,560 --> 00:03:36,360
They did find a tentative sign

63
00:03:36,360 --> 00:03:40,720
that hiring of younger workers had slowed
in some exposed occupations.

64
00:03:40,720 --> 00:03:45,467
The researchers could not establish from
that signal alone that AI caused it.

65
00:03:45,467 --> 00:03:47,840
The International Labour Organization
estimates

66
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:53,400
that roughly one in four jobs worldwide
has some exposure to generative AI.

67
00:03:53,440 --> 00:03:57,040
That means the technology could affect
tasks within those jobs.

68
00:03:57,040 --> 00:04:00,213
It does not mean one in four workers will
be fired.

69
00:04:00,213 --> 00:04:01,760
But a change in the work doesn't

70
00:04:01,760 --> 00:04:04,960
have to erase a profession to disrupt
someone's life.

71
00:04:04,960 --> 00:04:06,960
Machine translation and post-editing

72
00:04:06,960 --> 00:04:10,560
existed long before today's generative AI
boom.

73
00:04:10,560 --> 00:04:14,320
Take Julian Pintat, a freelance technical
translator.

74
00:04:14,320 --> 00:04:17,067
In a personal account written in 2025,

75
00:04:17,067 --> 00:04:20,240
he described going entire months without
work.

76
00:04:20,240 --> 00:04:24,680
By May, he said, most requests were for
reviewing text produced by machine

77
00:04:25,080 --> 00:04:29,840
translation, work he regarded as much less
well paid than translating from scratch.

78
00:04:29,840 --> 00:04:31,920
His story didn't end there.

79
00:04:31,920 --> 00:04:35,760
By that autumn, he wrote that clients and
conventional translation assignments

80
00:04:35,760 --> 00:04:40,320
had returned, although his business had
not yet recovered to its former level.

81
00:04:40,320 --> 00:04:42,920
This is one person's account, not proof

82
00:04:42,920 --> 00:04:48,000
of what happened across an entire industry
or why each client made its decisions.

83
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:49,640
But it shows how uncertain

84
00:04:49,640 --> 00:04:53,440
and uneven a transition can feel to the
person living through it.

85
00:04:53,440 --> 00:04:56,320
Because a job is more than a list of
tasks.

86
00:04:56,320 --> 00:05:01,000
It is an income, a way to build skills,
and often a way to feel useful.

87
00:05:01,120 --> 00:05:03,280
Change enough of the tasks, quickly

88
00:05:03,280 --> 00:05:07,480
enough, and you may change the whole
bargain between people and employers.

89
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:09,880
One possible future is a good one.

90
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:13,160
Workers use AI to do more, businesses

91
00:05:13,320 --> 00:05:18,120
expand, new roles emerge, and some of the
gains return to the people doing the work.

92
00:05:18,680 --> 00:05:20,760
But another future is possible too.

93
00:05:21,200 --> 00:05:23,520
The technology becomes more productive

94
00:05:23,520 --> 00:05:27,160
while the people whose work has changed
struggle to share in the benefits.

95
00:05:27,680 --> 00:05:30,040
A new job may exist, but in another

96
00:05:30,080 --> 00:05:34,680
city, requiring experience or training the
person losing work does not have.

97
00:05:35,160 --> 00:05:37,040
And if many households earn less

98
00:05:37,040 --> 00:05:40,280
while the gains flow to a small group,
there is another question.

99
00:05:40,800 --> 00:05:44,520
Who buys the goods and services all this
new productivity creates?

100
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:46,760
That isn't a prediction of collapse.

101
00:05:47,080 --> 00:05:51,867
It is a reason to ask how the benefits and
costs of a transition are distributed.

102
00:05:51,867 --> 00:05:54,520
The effects could reach beyond a paycheck.

103
00:05:54,560 --> 00:05:56,160
If entry-level work becomes harder

104
00:05:56,160 --> 00:06:01,600
to find, some people may postpone moving
out, starting a family, or retraining.

105
00:06:01,600 --> 00:06:03,200
Others might gain time,

106
00:06:03,200 --> 00:06:07,320
better tools, or access to work they could
never have done before.

107
00:06:07,360 --> 00:06:12,160
The same technology can open a door for
one person and narrow it for another.

108
00:06:12,160 --> 00:06:14,560
And AI may keep improving.

109
00:06:14,560 --> 00:06:16,240
We do not know how quickly,

110
00:06:16,240 --> 00:06:20,960
which tasks will become reliable, or how
companies and governments will respond.

111
00:06:20,960 --> 00:06:22,800
A machine that can produce an impressive

112
00:06:22,800 --> 00:06:27,520
answer is not automatically ready to take
responsibility for the consequences.

113
00:06:27,520 --> 00:06:32,480
Who checks it, who owns it, and who
benefits from it are human decisions.

114
00:06:32,480 --> 00:06:37,360
That is why the most important question
may not be whether AI can do more work.

115
00:06:37,360 --> 00:06:41,600
The question is what kind of world we
build around that ability.

116
00:06:41,600 --> 00:06:45,360
Will companies invest in training people
alongside their machines?

117
00:06:45,360 --> 00:06:48,960
Will workers have time and support to move
into new roles?

118
00:06:48,960 --> 00:06:51,227
Will the gains make life more secure,

119
00:06:51,227 --> 00:06:54,267
or only make the race to keep up more
exhausting?

120
00:06:54,267 --> 00:06:56,640
There is another part to Kiran's story.

121
00:06:56,640 --> 00:06:58,560
In October 2026,

122
00:06:58,560 --> 00:07:03,280
Business Insider reported that she had
landed her first software engineering job.

123
00:07:03,280 --> 00:07:07,840
Her experience is one person's story, not
a measure of the whole market.

124
00:07:07,840 --> 00:07:09,360
It doesn't prove why her search

125
00:07:09,360 --> 00:07:12,800
was difficult, and it doesn't settle what
happens next.

126
00:07:12,800 --> 00:07:14,520
But it brings us back to the question

127
00:07:14,520 --> 00:07:17,840
she faced at the beginning: how does
someone get the experience

128
00:07:17,840 --> 00:07:22,640
a career demands if the opportunities to
gain it become harder to find?

129
00:07:22,640 --> 00:07:25,440
The career ladder is not a law of nature.

130
00:07:25,440 --> 00:07:28,587
People built it, and people can rebuild
it.

131
00:07:28,587 --> 00:07:32,960
But first we have to notice what happens
if its lowest rung starts to disappear.
